// pass_table.def — the ONE declarative table of every GPU command the sim
// records, and the thing Simulation::Encode* iterates to record it.
//
// WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
// --------------------
// Phase 2b of docs/PLAN_vulkan_port.md. Dawn generates every hazard barrier in
// the tick automatically from bind-group usage. Vulkan does not: phase 3
// hand-writes ~60 read-after-write hops on one 512 MiB buffer, and a barrier
// that is too weak is not a crash — it is a race whose outcome depends on how a
// particular GPU schedules workgroups, i.e. a world hash that matches on the
// RTX 3060 Ti and diverges on the next vendor (CLAUDE.md rule 1).
//
// The design thesis (docs/vulkan_barrier_graph.md §1.1) is that barriers are
// GENERATED from this table and never hand-written at a call site. That is only
// worth anything if the table is FAITHFUL — if it says a pass reads `voxels`
// when it does not, phase 3 emits a barrier for a hazard that is not there
// (harmless); if it OMITS a read that happens, phase 3 emits nothing and the
// hazard is unsynchronized (a silent cross-vendor desync).
//
// Two mechanisms keep it faithful, and both depend on this being ONE list:
//
//   1. The recorder iterates it. Simulation::EncodeTick and friends do not
//      record commands directly any more — they walk the rows below. A row that
//      is wrong about its pipeline or its dispatch is wrong about what actually
//      runs, so the selftest catches it. The table cannot drift from the
//      recording because it IS the recording.
//   2. scripts/check_pass_table.py diffs each row's declared R/W sets against
//      the bindings its WGSL entry point actually touches, via a call-graph walk
//      ROOTED at that entry point (docs/vulkan_barrier_graph.md §6.1). It runs
//      from the PostToolUse hook on every .wgsl and on this file.
//
// Same play as src/sim/tuning_params.def: two lists that must agree are a
// silent bug, one list plus a generator is not. And the same standing
// obligation — a tick-path edit that changes a kernel's bindings updates this
// file in the same commit, or the checker fails (barrier_graph §8).
//
// HOW TO READ A ROW
// -----------------
//   PASS(name, passGroup, table, pipe, kind, dispatch..., groups, dynOff,
//        cond, repeat, uses)
//
//   name      stable identity.
//   passGroup which ComputePassEncoder the row is recorded into, and the label
//             that pass opens with (the PassTimer label under --measure).
//             Consecutive compute rows sharing a group string go into ONE pass,
//             exactly as the hand-written recorder did; nullptr on Fill/Copy
//             rows, which are encoder-level and end any open pass. The splits
//             are the thing phase 2b must NOT change, so they are stated rather
//             than inferred from adjacency.
//   table     which command buffer this row belongs to (PT_TICK, PT_WORLDGEN,
//             PT_GENLIST, PT_LOADRESET, PT_HASHONLY, PT_FARFILL). One table per
//             Encode* entry point, per barrier_graph §2.5's "a code path that
//             submits a command buffer gets a table".
//   pipe      PIPE_* selector into Simulation's pipeline members; PIPE_NONE for
//             Fill/Copy rows.
//   kind      K_COMPUTE (direct dispatch), K_INDIRECT (DispatchWorkgroupsIndirect),
//             K_FILL (ClearBuffer), K_COPY (CopyBufferToBuffer).
//   groups    GRP_SIM (simBG_ + dynamic passUBO offset), GRP_SLIM_PART
//             (simSlimBG_ + particleBG_), GRP_SLIM_FAR (simSlimBG_ + farBG_),
//             GRP_NONE for Fill/Copy.
//   cond      C_* predicate, evaluated on the CPU before recording. A row whose
//             condition is false is SKIPPED ENTIRELY — it records nothing and
//             (phase 3) touches no buffer's last-access state. barrier_graph
//             §3.9/§7.5: this is the only correct handling, and it is why
//             barriers must be computed at record time against live state and
//             never precomputed per adjacent table-index pair.
//   repeat    1 normally. 54 on the CA row — the 27 colors x 2 gravity
//             substeps, dispatched with a per-iteration dynamic passUBO offset.
//
// USES: R(b) read, W(b) written-not-read, RW(b) read+written, A(b) atomic RMW
// (READ|WRITE for barrier purposes — Vulkan has no atomic-specific access
// flag), U(b) uniform read, I(b) indirect-args read, TR(b) transfer read,
// TW(b) transfer write.
//
// `uses` is the union of the bindings the ENTRY POINT actually touches, NOT the
// bind-group layout. simBGL_ binds 17 buffers; sim_step.wgsl declares 8 and the
// table lists 8. Declaring the whole layout would insert barriers on occupancy,
// pick, hash and cellOps in the CA loop that are pure cost with no hazard
// behind them. The checker asserts layout ⊇ used, never layout == used.
//
// THE CA ROW IS NOT AN OPTIMIZATION TARGET. Its 54 iterations must never
// overlap, and that is a requirement of the 3x3x3 COLOR LATTICE, not of memory
// visibility (barrier_graph §3.6/§7.1). Within one dispatch every acting cell
// shares one colorPhase, so acting cells are >=3 apart on every axis while
// writes reach <=1 cell, and destination writes are provably disjoint. That
// disjointness holds within a color phase and nowhere else. Two iterations
// running concurrently are two DIFFERENT colors running concurrently, and cells
// of adjacent colors are adjacent by construction: their writes collide and the
// winner is whichever workgroup arrived first — which CLAUDE.md rule 1 bans by
// name. Batching iterations, merging "redundant" barriers between them, or
// splitting them across queues is the removal of rule 1, not a perf win.
//
// clang-format off

// ============================================================ PT_TICK ======
// Simulation::EncodeTick. Order below is the record order, verbatim.
// barrier_graph §2.4.

// ---- phase 0: pre-pass fills (simulation.cpp, top of EncodeTick) ----------
// DirtyOut is cleared every tick: it accumulates "written this tick" marks.
// argsStage is the compaction target; note the fill only zeroes the COUNT —
// sim_compact atomicStores args[1] and args[2] itself, so dispatchArgs.y/.z
// come from the SHADER. The fill->dispatch WAW is therefore load-bearing for
// dispatch VALIDITY, not merely count accuracy: lose it and the args read
// {count, 0, 0}, i.e. zero workgroups and a silently frozen world
// (barrier_graph §7.3).
PASS(fill_dirtyOut, nullptr, PT_TICK, PIPE_NONE, K_FILL, 0,0,0, GRP_NONE, DYN_NONE, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(TW(DirtyOut)))
PASS(fill_argsStage, nullptr, PT_TICK, PIPE_NONE, K_FILL, 0,0,0, GRP_NONE, DYN_NONE, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(TW(ArgsStage)))
PASS(fill_claim, nullptr, PT_TICK, PIPE_NONE, K_FILL, 0,0,0, GRP_NONE, DYN_NONE, C_PARTICLES, 1,
     USES(TW(Claim)))
PASS(fill_expMask, nullptr, PT_TICK, PIPE_NONE, K_FILL, 0,0,0, GRP_NONE, DYN_NONE, C_EXP, 1,
     USES(TW(ExpMask)))
PASS(fill_hash, nullptr, PT_TICK, PIPE_NONE, K_FILL, 0,0,0, GRP_NONE, DYN_NONE, C_HASH, 1,
     USES(TW(Hash)))
PASS(fill_actVoxViz, nullptr, PT_TICK, PIPE_NONE, K_FILL, 0,0,0, GRP_NONE, DYN_NONE, C_VIZACTIVE, 1,
     USES(TW(ActVoxViz)))

// ---- phase 1: prep (one ComputePassEncoder today: mutate+explode+compact) --
// D_OPS/D_CELLS/D_EXP/D_SPAWN are dispatch-extent selectors resolved from the
// tick's counts at record time (see PassDispatchExtent in pass_table.cpp).
PASS(mutate, "prep(mutate+explode+compact)", PT_TICK, PIPE_MUTATE,      K_COMPUTE, D_OPS,4,4,   GRP_SIM,       DYN_ZERO, C_OPS, 1,
     USES(R(Materials) U(TickUBO) R(OpsBuf) RW(Voxels) A(DirtyIn) A(DirtyOut)
          R(PageTable) A(PageFaults)))
// No R(Materials): `cells` writes the authored word straight into the grid and
// never consults the material table (unlike `main`, which does). The barrier
// doc's T11 row lists materials; the rooted walk says otherwise and the walk is
// right — check_pass_table.py caught this.
PASS(mutateCells, "prep(mutate+explode+compact)", PT_TICK, PIPE_MUTATE_CELLS,K_COMPUTE, D_CELLS,1,1, GRP_SIM,       DYN_ZERO, C_CELLS, 1,
     USES(U(TickUBO) R(CellOps) RW(Voxels) A(DirtyIn) A(DirtyOut)
          R(PageTable) A(PageFaults)))
// WIND PRIMITIVE FOOTPRINT WAKE (docs/RESEARCH_wind.md §4.3, §10). Sets the
// dirty flag on the chunks a fan/gust/tornado declared this tick, so a SETTLED
// dune under one of them is simulated at all. It writes no voxel and reads no
// voxel: the whole row is two atomic stores per listed chunk.
//
// Placed HERE, after the mutation rows and before `compact`, because the
// compaction is what turns dirty flags into this tick's dispatch list — a wake
// recorded after it would take effect one tick late, which reads as a fan with
// lag. The two atomic uses are what generate the barrier against compact's
// read of DirtyIn.
PASS(windWake, "prep(mutate+explode+compact)", PT_TICK, PIPE_WIND_WAKE, K_COMPUTE, D_WINDWAKE,1,1, GRP_SIM, DYN_ZERO, C_WINDWAKE, 1,
     USES(U(TickUBO) A(DirtyIn) A(DirtyOut)))
// mark reads the neighborhood, apply writes it. The two-phase split is what
// makes the explosion kernel race-free (CLAUDE.md build gotchas); it only works
// if the barrier between them exists, which under Dawn was free.
// read-only in practice: `voxels` is read_write for the module because `apply`
// writes it; `mark` is the pure-read half of that split and must stay so — a
// kernel that both reads and writes this neighborhood races itself.
PASS(explodeMark, "prep(mutate+explode+compact)", PT_TICK, PIPE_EXPLODE_MARK, K_COMPUTE, D_EXP,D_EXPWG,D_EXPWG,  GRP_SLIM_PART, DYN_NONE, C_EXP, 1,
     USES(R(Voxels) R(Materials) U(TickUBO) R(ExpOps) W(ExpMask) A(DirtyIn) A(DirtyOut)
          R(PageTable)))
// read-only in practice: `expMask` is read_write for the module because `mark`
// writes it. `apply` consumes the mask `mark` produced and never amends it.
PASS(explodeApply, "prep(mutate+explode+compact)", PT_TICK, PIPE_EXPLODE_APPLY,K_COMPUTE, D_EXP,D_EXPWG,D_EXPWG,  GRP_SLIM_PART, DYN_NONE, C_EXP, 1,
     USES(R(Materials) U(TickUBO) R(ExpOps) R(ExpMask) RW(Voxels) A(DirtyIn) A(DirtyOut)
          A(ParticleCounts) W(ParticlesRead) R(PageTable) A(PageFaults)))
// CPU particle spawns (debris shatter) append to the READ page here, before
// args1 sizes the integrate dispatch — same page explosion ejecta uses.
PASS(particleSpawn, "prep(mutate+explode+compact)", PT_TICK, PIPE_P_SPAWN,     K_COMPUTE, D_SPAWN,1,1,GRP_SLIM_PART, DYN_NONE, C_SPAWN, 1,
     USES(R(SpawnOps) U(TickUBO) A(ParticleCounts) W(ParticlesRead)))
// compact dirtyIn -> dense chunk list + indirect args, AFTER mutate/explode so
// freshly touched chunks simulate this tick.
// C_CAACTIVE, not C_ALWAYS: this row exists ONLY to build the CA's dispatch
// list, so when the CA is skipped it is pure cost (a 512-workgroup scan of all
// 32,768 dirty flags). The three rows below it — the staging copy and the 54
// CA iterations — form one unit that is recorded or skipped together, and they
// share the condition rather than each testing a variant of it. §3.4.
//
// fill_argsStage above STAYS C_ALWAYS deliberately: argsStage is the shared
// compaction target for BOTH compact (here) and compactNext (phase 5b), and
// phase 5b re-fills it anyway. Zeroing a 12-byte buffer is free; making its
// fill conditional would couple two phases that are otherwise independent.
PASS(compact, "prep(mutate+explode+compact)", PT_TICK, PIPE_COMPACT,      K_COMPUTE, D_CHUNKS64,1,1, GRP_SIM, DYN_ZERO, C_CAACTIVE, 1,
     USES(R(DirtyIn) W(DirtyList) A(ArgsStage)))

// ---- phase 2: indirect staging -------------------------------------------
// The indirect-only args buffer is never bound in any bind group: Dawn forbids
// indirect + bound-writable usage of one buffer in the same pass, and
// sim_step.wgsl deliberately omits binding 13 to keep argsStage outside the CA
// pass's usage scope. v1 keeps the staging copy (barrier_graph §4.10).
// C_CAACTIVE with compact: this copy stages the count compact just produced.
// With compact skipped there is nothing new to stage, and copying the zeroed
// argsStage would write {0,0,0} into dispatchArgs — which phase 5b's own copy
// then overwrites anyway. Skipping both keeps dispatchArgs holding the last
// real value, which nothing reads while the CA rows are absent.
PASS(copy_argsStage_dispatchArgs, nullptr, PT_TICK, PIPE_NONE, K_COPY, 0,0,12, GRP_NONE, DYN_NONE, C_CAACTIVE, 1,
     USES(TR(ArgsStage) TW(DispatchArgs)))

// ---- phase 3: the CA loop -------------------------------------------------
// 27 colors x 2 gravity substeps, one workgroup per dirty chunk. A settled
// world dispatches zero workgroups here (DESIGN.md §11). DYN_CA feeds iteration
// k the passUBO slice at k * kPassStride — which is where colorPhase and
// substep come from, i.e. what makes each iteration a DIFFERENT color. See the
// lattice note in this file's header before touching repeat.
//
// dirtyList is declared read_write at binding 12 in sim_step.wgsl but the entry
// point only READS it (dirtyList[wg.x] at the top of main).
// read-only in practice: declared read_write only because WGSL cannot mix
// access modes for one binding across the module's entry points.
// The three MLS-MPM buffers are the excited-fluid coupling (DESIGN.md §5):
// doReactions synthesizes an excited cell as a liquid neighbour from LAST
// tick's block map + node mass + the seam's intent word, and flags the cell
// for consumption when a rule takes it. All three are last-tick state during
// the whole 54-iteration loop — no intra-loop hazard, one inter-table one.
PASS(ca, "ca(54 color x substep)", PT_TICK, PIPE_STEP, K_INDIRECT, IND_DISPATCHARGS,0,0, GRP_SIM, DYN_CA, C_CAACTIVE, 54,
     USES(R(Materials) U(TickUBO) U(PassUBO) R(Reactions) R(DirtyList) I(DispatchArgs)
          RW(Voxels) A(DirtyOut) A(Support) R(PageTable) A(PageFaults)
          R(FluidBlockMap) R(FluidGrid) A(FluidCellScratch) A(ActVoxViz)))

// ---- phase 3b: water bodies (docs/PLAN_water_master.md M2) ----------------
//
// The drain ledger and the surface shave. Recorded AFTER the CA and before the
// particles for the same reason the seam is recorded after the CA: these are
// post-CA writers, their voxel writes are next tick's business, and they mark
// DirtyOut exactly the way sim_particle's resolve does.
//
// THE ROW ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING and sim_waterbody.wgsl's header is the argument
// for it: quiet measures, the ledger consumes LAST tick's shave report and
// publishes THIS tick's instruction, the reduce fills the accumulators the
// ledger just armed, and the shave acts and reports. Reordering them silently
// changes the cadence from mark/apply to read-your-own-write.
//
// C_WATERBODY is `waterChunkCount > 0`, which is 0 whenever sim.waterBodyMode
// is 0 — so the off switch is "no row is recorded", and the pinned world hash
// cannot move at the shipping default. That is the whole reason M1 and M2 could
// land without a rebaseline.
//
// R(DirtyIn): quiescence reads THIS tick's dirty set — the CA's own answer to
// "was this chunk disturbed", which is what replaced M1's snapshot read and the
// rule-1 hazard that came with it (DESIGN.md §5b.4).
PASS(waterQuiet, "waterBody", PT_TICK, PIPE_WATER_QUIET, K_COMPUTE, D_WATERCHUNKS64,1,1, GRP_SIM, DYN_ZERO, C_WATERBODY, 1,
     USES(U(TickUBO) R(DirtyIn) R(FluidBlockMap) A(WaterBodyState)))
// Pure arithmetic on the ledger: one thread per body, no voxel, no chunk.
// ONE workgroup of 64 threads covers kWaterBodyCap bodies exactly. That
// coupling is why the shader tests `b >= WATERBODY_CAP` from the prelude
// rather than trusting the extent: raise the cap past 64 and this literal is
// the second place to change, and the guard is what makes the miss a missing
// body rather than an out-of-bounds atomic.
PASS(waterLedger, "waterBody", PT_TICK, PIPE_WATER_LEDGER, K_COMPUTE, 1,1,1, GRP_SIM, DYN_ZERO, C_WATERBODY, 1,
     USES(U(TickUBO) A(WaterBodyState)))
// ---- M3: THE DISCHARGE, EMITTED (component 6) ---------------------------
// One thread per RESERVED spawn-op slot, filling the block the CPU charged
// before the command buffer existed. W(FluidSpawnOps) is the row that matters:
// this is the ONLY GPU writer of that buffer, `seam_spawn` in PT_FLUIDSEAM is
// the reader, and PT_FLUIDSEAM is recorded after PT_TICK — so the barrier
// between the two is derived rather than assumed. Omit the W and the jet is a
// race whose symptom is missing water on some drivers and not others.
//
// It reads nothing of the world: the head, the discharge and the exit speed
// were evaluated ONCE in the ledger row above and published into
// waterBodyState (plan §6's single-evaluation rule). There is no second rule
// here to disagree with the first.
PASS(waterDrain, "waterBody", PT_TICK, PIPE_WATER_DRAIN, K_COMPUTE, D_WATERDRAIN,1,1, GRP_SIM, DYN_ZERO, C_WATERDRAIN, 1,
     USES(U(TickUBO) A(WaterBodyState) W(FluidSpawnOps)))
// The ONE whole-footprint pass, and it runs on the single tick a body spends in
// WB_MEASURING.
// read-only in practice: `voxels` is read_write for the module because wbShave
// writes it. wbReduce only sums fullness — adoption reads the voxel sum INTO
// the ledger and writes nothing back (plan §3.1: the voxels are authoritative,
// the descriptor is a cache of aggregates over them).
PASS(waterReduce, "waterBody", PT_TICK, PIPE_WATER_REDUCE, K_COMPUTE, D_WATERCHUNKS,1,1, GRP_SIM, DYN_ZERO, C_WATERBODY, 1,
     USES(U(TickUBO) R(Voxels) R(PageTable) A(WaterBodyState)))
// The only voxel WRITER in the subsystem. A(PageFaults) because it goes through
// voxStore, which is the engine's one write seam and the probe that names a
// dropped word and the chunk that refused it — the shave's chunks are declared
// as op targets CPU-side (support.cpp, the windWake precedent) precisely so
// that probe stays at zero.
PASS(waterShave, "waterBody", PT_TICK, PIPE_WATER_SHAVE, K_COMPUTE, D_WATERCHUNKS,1,1, GRP_SIM, DYN_ZERO, C_WATERBODY, 1,
     USES(U(TickUBO) RW(Voxels) A(DirtyOut) R(PageTable) A(PageFaults)
          A(WaterBodyState)))
// ---- M3: HOLE DETECTION (component 6's trigger) -------------------------
// LAST, and after the shave on purpose: it reports into the *N half of the
// hole record and the ledger promotes that NEXT tick (plan §3.3 — never read a
// tally in the pass that writes it), so running it here means the level it
// tests against is the one this tick actually settled on.
//
// R(DirtyIn) + R(FluidBlockMap) are not incidental, they are the ENTIRE cost
// model: this pass returns immediately on a listed chunk the CA did not mark
// and the MPM has no block in, which is every chunk of a still lake. Holes
// appear when someone digs, and digging dirties a chunk.
// read-only in practice: `voxels` is read_write for the module because wbShave
// and wbDrain write (voxels / the op stream); wbHole only asks whether a cell
// and the cell under it are air. Declaring it a read is what keeps the CA loop
// from taking 54 extra WAW barriers for a pass that writes nothing.
PASS(waterHole, "waterBody", PT_TICK, PIPE_WATER_HOLE, K_COMPUTE, D_WATERCHUNKS,1,1, GRP_SIM, DYN_ZERO, C_WATERBODY, 1,
     USES(U(TickUBO) R(Voxels) R(DirtyIn) R(FluidBlockMap) R(PageTable)
          A(WaterBodyState)))
// ---- M5: THE SCHEDULED CONTAINER SWEEP (components 2 case 2 + 10) --------
// LAST in the block, and after the hole scan for the reason the hole scan is
// after the shave: the level these two measure against is the one this tick
// actually settled on, and their outputs are read NEXT tick by the ledger
// (plan section 3.3 at pass granularity).
//
// C_WATERSWEEP, not C_WATERBODY: at most ONE basin re-derives per tick, and
// only a basin someone has dug into is ever scheduled. A still lake records
// neither row, which is what keeps `--gate waterbody` pass E's zero-at-rest a
// property of the design rather than of a threshold.
//
// read-only in practice: `voxels` is read_write for the module because wbShave
// and wbDrain write (voxels / the op stream); wbSweep only asks what material a
// cell holds. It writes nothing at all outside its own region of
// waterBodyState — no A(DirtyOut), no A(PageFaults), because it stores no
// voxel. Declaring it a read is what keeps the CA loop from taking 54 extra WAW
// barriers for a pass that writes nothing.
PASS(waterSweep, "waterBody", PT_TICK, PIPE_WATER_SWEEP, K_COMPUTE, D_WATERCHUNKS,1,1, GRP_SIM, DYN_ZERO, C_WATERSWEEP, 1,
     USES(U(TickUBO) R(Voxels) R(PageTable) A(WaterBodyState)))
// ONE workgroup. The label propagation is a fixpoint over workgroup memory
// with barriers between its read and write phases — deterministic without
// atomicCAS, which rule 1 bans. It reads the openness bitmap wbSweep just
// wrote, so the A->A barrier the recorder derives between these two rows is
// load-bearing rather than incidental.
PASS(waterSplit, "waterBody", PT_TICK, PIPE_WATER_SPLIT, K_COMPUTE, 1,1,1, GRP_SIM, DYN_ZERO, C_WATERSWEEP, 1,
     USES(U(TickUBO) A(WaterBodyState)))

// ---- phase 4: particles ---------------------------------------------------
// Runs after the CA so flights see settled ground. Grid writes land in
// dirtyOut, which the occupancy update below already covers.
PASS(particleArgs1, "particleArgs1", PT_TICK, PIPE_P_ARGS1, K_COMPUTE, 1,1,1, GRP_SLIM_PART, DYN_NONE, C_PARTICLES, 1,
     USES(A(ParticleCounts) U(TickUBO) W(PArgsStage)))
PASS(copy_pArgs_pDispatch1, nullptr, PT_TICK, PIPE_NONE, K_COPY, 16,0,12, GRP_NONE, DYN_NONE, C_PARTICLES, 1,
     USES(TR(PArgsStage) TW(PDispatchArgs)))
// No A(DirtyOut): `markDirtyNext` is the only writer of dirtyOut in this module
// and it is called only from `resolve` (sim_particle.wgsl:242, :265), never from
// `integrate`. The barrier doc's T42 row lists dirtyOut; the rooted walk says
// otherwise and the walk is right — check_pass_table.py caught this.
// read-only in practice: `voxels` is read_write for the module because
// `resolve` writes it when a particle rejoins the grid. `integrate` only tests
// cells for blockage along the flight path.
PASS(particleIntegrate, "particleIntegrate+args2", PT_TICK, PIPE_P_INTEGRATE, K_INDIRECT, IND_PDISPATCHARGS,0,0,
     GRP_SLIM_PART, DYN_NONE, C_PARTICLES, 1,
     USES(R(Voxels) R(Materials) U(TickUBO) RW(ParticlesRead) I(PDispatchArgs)
          W(ParticlesWrite) A(ParticleCounts) A(Claim) R(PageTable)))
PASS(particleArgs2, "particleIntegrate+args2", PT_TICK, PIPE_P_ARGS2, K_COMPUTE, 1,1,1, GRP_SLIM_PART, DYN_NONE, C_PARTICLES, 1,
     USES(A(ParticleCounts) U(TickUBO) W(PArgsStage)))
// This second copy overwrites the args the integrate dispatch above FETCHED —
// a WAR against an in-flight indirect read, which is the case an implementer
// skips ("nothing reads it after the write"). barrier_graph §7.2.
PASS(copy_pArgs_pDispatch2, nullptr, PT_TICK, PIPE_NONE, K_COPY, 16,0,12, GRP_NONE, DYN_NONE, C_PARTICLES, 1,
     USES(TR(PArgsStage) TW(PDispatchArgs)))
PASS(copy_pArgs_drawArgs, nullptr, PT_TICK, PIPE_NONE, K_COPY, 0,0,16, GRP_NONE, DYN_NONE, C_PARTICLES, 1,
     USES(TR(PArgsStage) TW(DrawArgs)))
PASS(particleResolve, "particleResolve", PT_TICK, PIPE_P_RESOLVE, K_INDIRECT, IND_PDISPATCHARGS,0,0,
     GRP_SLIM_PART, DYN_NONE, C_PARTICLES, 1,
     USES(R(Materials) U(TickUBO) A(Claim) A(ParticleCounts) I(PDispatchArgs)
          RW(Voxels) RW(ParticlesWrite) A(DirtyOut) R(PageTable) A(PageFaults)))

// ---- phase 5a: hash tick (whole-world scan; also refreshes occupancy) ------
PASS(occupancyFull, "occupancyFull+pick(hashTick)", PT_TICK, PIPE_OCCUPANCY, K_COMPUTE, D_CHUNKS,1,1, GRP_SIM, DYN_ZERO, C_HASH, 1,
     USES(R(Voxels) R(Materials) U(TickUBO) W(Occupancy) A(Hash) R(PageTable)))
PASS(pick_hash, "occupancyFull+pick(hashTick)", PT_TICK, PIPE_PICK,      K_COMPUTE, 1,1,1,       GRP_SIM, DYN_ZERO, C_HASH, 1,
     USES(R(Voxels) R(Materials) U(RenderUBO) W(Pick) R(PageTable)))

// ---- phase 5b: dirty tick -------------------------------------------------
// Occupancy update over only the chunks written this tick: compact the dirtyOut
// flags (a superset of every voxel write) and dispatch indirect.
PASS(fill_argsStage2, nullptr, PT_TICK, PIPE_NONE, K_FILL, 0,0,0, GRP_NONE, DYN_NONE, C_DIRTYTICK, 1,
     USES(TW(ArgsStage)))
PASS(compactNext, "compactNext", PT_TICK, PIPE_COMPACT_NEXT, K_COMPUTE, D_CHUNKS64,1,1, GRP_SIM, DYN_ZERO, C_DIRTYTICK, 1,
     USES(R(DirtyOut) W(DirtyList) A(ArgsStage)))
PASS(copy_argsStage_dispatchArgs2, nullptr, PT_TICK, PIPE_NONE, K_COPY, 0,0,12, GRP_NONE, DYN_NONE, C_DIRTYTICK, 1,
     USES(TR(ArgsStage) TW(DispatchArgs)))
// dirtyList declared read_write at binding 12 in sim_occupancy.wgsl, read only
// by mainDirty.
// read-only in practice: shared module-scope declaration with the compaction
// entry points, which do write it.
PASS(occupancyDirty, "occupancyDirty+pick", PT_TICK, PIPE_OCCUPANCY_DIRTY, K_INDIRECT, IND_DISPATCHARGS,0,0,
     GRP_SIM, DYN_ZERO, C_DIRTYTICK, 1,
     USES(R(Voxels) R(Materials) R(DirtyList) I(DispatchArgs) W(Occupancy)
          R(PageTable)))
PASS(pick_dirty, "occupancyDirty+pick", PT_TICK, PIPE_PICK, K_COMPUTE, 1,1,1, GRP_SIM, DYN_ZERO, C_DIRTYTICK, 1,
     USES(R(Voxels) R(Materials) U(RenderUBO) W(Pick) R(PageTable)))
// Far-field phase 2: downsample the same compacted dirtyOut list into the
// cascades, so edits stay visible after the player walks away (render-only
// derived data — DESIGN.md §9). Rides the SAME indirect args as the occupancy
// update, so a settled world dispatches nothing. Separate pass: it uses farPL_
// (slim group 0 + far group 1), not simPL_.
// NOTE: hash ticks skip this — they take the whole-world occupancy branch and
// never compact dirtyOut, so there is no work list. A chunk edited on a hash
// tick is still dirty the next tick and downsamples then.
// read-only in practice: `voxels` is read_write for the module because
// `genChunk` (main/list) writes it. `fardown` only samples the live grid to
// downsample into the cascades — far-field is derived, render-only data and
// never writes back (DESIGN.md §9).
PASS(farDown, "farDown", PT_TICK, PIPE_FAR_DOWN, K_INDIRECT, IND_DISPATCHARGS,0,0,
     GRP_SLIM_FAR, DYN_NONE, C_DIRTYTICK, 1,
     USES(R(Voxels) R(Materials) U(TickUBO) U(FarUBO) R(DirtyList) I(DispatchArgs)
          A(FarVox) A(FarOcc) R(PageTable) R(TreeAtlas)))

// (The MLS-MPM spawn row moved into PT_FLUIDSEAM: with settle deleting
// particles and excite creating them GPU-side, appends go through the seam's
// compaction-owned count, not a CPU base.)

// ========================================================= PT_WORLDGEN =====
// Simulation::EncodeWorldgen. barrier_graph §2.5.1. The fills of dirty[0]/[1]
// DO hazard against the dispatch — genChunk atomicStores both — so "no hazards
// among them" applies only to the fills against each other.
PASS(wg_fill_dirty0, nullptr, PT_WORLDGEN, PIPE_NONE, K_FILL, 0,0,0, GRP_NONE, DYN_NONE, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(TW(Dirty0)))
PASS(wg_fill_dirty1, nullptr, PT_WORLDGEN, PIPE_NONE, K_FILL, 0,0,0, GRP_NONE, DYN_NONE, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(TW(Dirty1)))
PASS(wg_fill_hash, nullptr, PT_WORLDGEN, PIPE_NONE, K_FILL, 0,0,0, GRP_NONE, DYN_NONE, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(TW(Hash)))
PASS(wg_fill_support, nullptr, PT_WORLDGEN, PIPE_NONE, K_FILL, 0,0,0, GRP_NONE, DYN_NONE, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(TW(Support)))
PASS(wg_fill_pCounts, nullptr, PT_WORLDGEN, PIPE_NONE, K_FILL, 0,0,0, GRP_NONE, DYN_NONE, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(TW(ParticleCounts)))
PASS(wg_fill_claim, nullptr, PT_WORLDGEN, PIPE_NONE, K_FILL, 0,0,0, GRP_NONE, DYN_NONE, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(TW(Claim)))
// no ghost particles: drawArgs retains the last active tick's instance count
PASS(wg_fill_drawArgs, nullptr, PT_WORLDGEN, PIPE_NONE, K_FILL, 0,0,0, GRP_NONE, DYN_NONE, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(TW(DrawArgs)))
// MLS-MPM fluid does not survive a regen: the GPU-owned live count, the
// per-slot calm counters and the block map all reset, or a stale count would
// resurrect last world's particles and a stale block map would wake water
// next to fluid that no longer exists.
PASS(wg_fill_fluidArgs, nullptr, PT_WORLDGEN, PIPE_NONE, K_FILL, 0,0,0, GRP_NONE, DYN_NONE, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(TW(FluidArgsStage)))
PASS(wg_fill_fluidCalm, nullptr, PT_WORLDGEN, PIPE_NONE, K_FILL, 0,0,0, GRP_NONE, DYN_NONE, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(TW(FluidCalm)))
PASS(wg_fill_fluidBlockMap, nullptr, PT_WORLDGEN, PIPE_NONE, K_FILL, 0,0,0, GRP_NONE, DYN_NONE, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(TW(FluidBlockMap)))
// one workgroup per slot chunk; occupancy + dirty flags computed in-kernel
// C_DENSEWG, not C_ALWAYS: under --residency paged this whole-world dispatch
// is replaced by batched worldgenList submits (PLAN_page_table.md §3.5c),
// because genChunk writes all 4,096 cells of every slot it touches and a
// kernel cannot allocate — all 32,768 at once would need a dense pool. The
// FILL rows above it still run in both modes: they clear the transient
// buffers (hash, support, particle counts, both dirty pages) that worldgen
// resets regardless of how the voxels get written.
PASS(worldgen, "worldgen", PT_WORLDGEN, PIPE_WORLDGEN, K_COMPUTE, D_CHUNKS,1,1, GRP_SIM, DYN_ZERO, C_DENSEWG, 1,
     USES(R(Materials) U(TickUBO) W(Voxels) W(Occupancy) A(DirtyIn) A(DirtyOut)
          R(PageTable) A(PageFaults) R(TreeAtlas)))

// ========================================================== PT_GENLIST =====
// Simulation::EncodeGenList — its own encoder, submitted MID-FRAME between
// ticks from Stream::FillSlots. Binds simBG_ (all 17 bindings) but
// worldgen:list/genChunk touch six of them; the `uses` set is the six.
// genChunk atomicStores both dirtyIn and dirtyOut while the concurrently
// in-flight tick's markDirty/markBoth atomically write the same two buffers —
// a cross-submit WAW against a shader atomic, covered by barrier_graph §3.4's
// head-of-command-buffer global barrier, not by anything in this row.
PASS(worldgenList, "worldgenList", PT_GENLIST, PIPE_WORLDGEN_LIST, K_COMPUTE, D_GENCOUNT,1,1,
     GRP_SIM, DYN_ZERO, C_GENCOUNT, 1,
     USES(R(GenList) R(Materials) U(TickUBO) W(Voxels) W(Occupancy) A(DirtyIn) A(DirtyOut)
          R(PageTable) A(PageFaults) R(TreeAtlas)))

// ========================================================= PT_PAGEFILL =====
// PageTable::DrainFills — materializes the JITTER pages of ONE tick, recorded
// at the HEAD of that tick's command buffer, before any row that reads voxels
// (§5.4's ordering, the same reason the vkCmdFillBuffer path is drained there).
//
// This is the fill a 32-bit pattern cannot express: a JITTER sentinel's words
// vary per cell, so the page is filled by a dispatch instead. EMPTY and UNIFORM
// still go through FillTracked and never reach this table.
//
// W(Voxels), not RW: the kernel only stores. It reads genList for the
// (slot, entry) pairs and TickUBO for origin/seed, and it deliberately does NOT
// touch Occupancy or the dirty buffers — materialization relocates the world,
// it does not change it (see the kernel's closing comment).
PASS(pageFill, "pageFill", PT_PAGEFILL, PIPE_PAGEFILL, K_COMPUTE, D_GENCOUNT,1,1,
     GRP_SIM, DYN_ZERO, C_GENCOUNT, 1,
     USES(R(PageFillList) U(TickUBO) W(Voxels) R(PageTable) A(PageFaults)))

// ======================================================== PT_LOADRESET =====
// Simulation::EncodeLoadReset. barrier_graph §2.5.3: clears transient state
// (hash/particles/claims) and rebuilds occupancy over freshly uploaded voxels.
// The caller has already written the voxel + dirty buffers (worldio.cpp) — one
// of the two sanctioned MutationQueue bypasses (CLAUDE.md rule 3).
PASS(lr_fill_hash, nullptr, PT_LOADRESET, PIPE_NONE, K_FILL, 0,0,0, GRP_NONE, DYN_NONE, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(TW(Hash)))
PASS(lr_fill_support, nullptr, PT_LOADRESET, PIPE_NONE, K_FILL, 0,0,0, GRP_NONE, DYN_NONE, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(TW(Support)))
PASS(lr_fill_pCounts, nullptr, PT_LOADRESET, PIPE_NONE, K_FILL, 0,0,0, GRP_NONE, DYN_NONE, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(TW(ParticleCounts)))
PASS(lr_fill_claim, nullptr, PT_LOADRESET, PIPE_NONE, K_FILL, 0,0,0, GRP_NONE, DYN_NONE, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(TW(Claim)))
PASS(lr_fill_fluidArgs, nullptr, PT_LOADRESET, PIPE_NONE, K_FILL, 0,0,0, GRP_NONE, DYN_NONE, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(TW(FluidArgsStage)))
PASS(lr_fill_fluidCalm, nullptr, PT_LOADRESET, PIPE_NONE, K_FILL, 0,0,0, GRP_NONE, DYN_NONE, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(TW(FluidCalm)))
PASS(lr_fill_fluidBlockMap, nullptr, PT_LOADRESET, PIPE_NONE, K_FILL, 0,0,0, GRP_NONE, DYN_NONE, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(TW(FluidBlockMap)))
PASS(lr_fill_drawArgs, nullptr, PT_LOADRESET, PIPE_NONE, K_FILL, 0,0,0, GRP_NONE, DYN_NONE, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(TW(DrawArgs)))
PASS(lr_occupancyFull, "occupancyFull(loadReset)", PT_LOADRESET, PIPE_OCCUPANCY, K_COMPUTE, D_CHUNKS,1,1,
     GRP_SIM, DYN_ZERO, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(R(Voxels) R(Materials) U(TickUBO) W(Occupancy) A(Hash) R(PageTable)))

// ========================================================= PT_HASHONLY =====
// Simulation::EncodeHashOnly — standalone whole-world hash pass (save/load
// verification). barrier_graph §2.5.4.
PASS(ho_fill_hash, nullptr, PT_HASHONLY, PIPE_NONE, K_FILL, 0,0,0, GRP_NONE, DYN_NONE, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(TW(Hash)))
PASS(ho_occupancyFull, "occupancyFull(hashOnly)", PT_HASHONLY, PIPE_OCCUPANCY, K_COMPUTE, D_CHUNKS,1,1,
     GRP_SIM, DYN_ZERO, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(R(Voxels) R(Materials) U(TickUBO) W(Occupancy) A(Hash) R(PageTable)))

// ========================================================== PT_FARFILL =====
// Simulation::EncodeFarFill. Encoded into the tick's command buffer AFTER
// EncodeTick today, and also driven standalone in a loop by --shot's far-field
// drain (barrier_graph §2.5.6). farCount travels INSIDE tickUBO, not as a
// dispatch dimension, so in the standalone loop the upload->dispatch RAW on
// tickUBO is load-bearing PER ITERATION.
//
// farVox/farOcc carry a WAW against farDown, which wrote them earlier in the
// same command buffer with a DIFFERENT atomic idiom (atomicAnd/atomicOr vs this
// row's full-word atomicStore). "They're both atomic" is not a reason to relax
// the barrier: atomics guarantee per-operation atomicity, not visibility of one
// dispatch's results to the next. barrier_graph §3.8/§7.8.
//
// FarPatch is uploaded by FarField::PrepareTick in the SAME tick, so its
// transfer->compute RAW is as load-bearing as farList's: the header pairs are
// indexed by dispatch entry and a stale one points the patch loop at another
// entry's payload.
PASS(farFill, "farFill", PT_FARFILL, PIPE_FAR_FILL, K_COMPUTE, D_FARCOUNT,1,1,
     GRP_SLIM_FAR, DYN_NONE, C_FARCOUNT, 1,
     USES(R(Materials) U(TickUBO) R(FarList) U(FarUBO) R(FarPatch)
          A(FarVox) A(FarOcc) R(TreeAtlas)))

// =========================================================== PT_FLUID ======
// ONE MLS-MPM SUBSTEP (docs/PLAN_mpm_fluids.md; sim_fluid.wgsl). Recorded
// kFluidSubsteps times per tick from EncodeTick, into the TICK's command
// buffer, only when fluid particles exist — a world that never placed the
// prototype fluid records none of this (rule 2). The spawn row is NOT here:
// it runs once per tick and lives at the end of PT_TICK below.
//
// The recorder's last-access tracker persists across RecordTable calls within
// one command buffer, so repeating this table generates the inter-substep
// barriers (grid WAW into the next clear, particle RAW into the next mark)
// exactly like intra-table ones. Determinism does not ride on any of these
// conditions: every row is C_ALWAYS within the table, and whether the table
// is recorded at all is a pure function of the CPU-owned particle count.
//
// The node ACCUMULATORS are rebuilt every substep (fluidClear below). The
// chunk->block MAP is not: it is built ONCE per tick, by PT_FLUIDMAP.
//
// ===================================================== PT_FLUIDMAP ========
// The chunk -> node-block map, built ONCE PER TICK between the seam's front
// half and the first substep (plan §7 item 4). fill zeroes the map, mark flags
// chunks under particle support (atomicOr — the final map state is
// order-independent), alloc assigns block indices by a single-workgroup scan in
// slot order (deterministic) and emits the node-pass dispatch args.
//
// WHY ONCE IS ENOUGH, and what pays for it: a particle's velocity is CFL-capped
// at 0.45 cell/substep, so it travels at most 0.45*sim.fluidSubsteps cells in a
// whole tick (4.05 at the 9-substep default, 2.7 at the historical 6). `mark`
// pads its support by FLUID_MARK_PAD = ceil(that) cells in every direction —
// derived from the same knob in common.wgsl, so the pad cannot fall behind a
// substep bump — and a particle can never reach a chunk this map does not
// already cover. (A particle whose chunk has no block simply
// freezes for the substep — deterministic, mass-preserving, and the same
// bounded degradation the kFluidBlocks budget already accepts — so the pad is
// belt AND braces, not the only guard.)
//
// It must run AFTER the seam: exciteEmit and spawnAppend both create particles,
// and the map has to cover them. It must run BEFORE the substeps for the
// obvious reason. And the seam's FRONT half deliberately reads the map from
// LAST tick (consumeApply addresses the CA's flags exactly as the CA wrote
// them), which is why this is a table of its own rather than rows appended to
// the seam.
PASS(fluid_fill_blockMap, nullptr, PT_FLUIDMAP, PIPE_NONE, K_FILL, 0,0,0, GRP_NONE, DYN_NONE, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(TW(FluidBlockMap)))
// read-only in practice: `fluidParticles` is read_write for the module because
// g2p writes it. mark only reads positions to flag chunks. The per-particle
// extent is GPU-owned now (fluidArgs[FA_LIVE], the seam's count), so mark and
// every other per-particle pass dispatches indirectly off fluidPDispatchArgs,
// and each kernel re-bounds itself on the live count it reads from fluidArgs.
PASS(fluidMark, "fluid(substep)", PT_FLUIDMAP, PIPE_FLUID_MARK, K_INDIRECT, IND_FLUIDPARGS,0,0, GRP_SLIM_FLUID, DYN_NONE, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(U(TickUBO) R(FluidParticlesWrite) A(FluidBlockMap) R(FluidArgsStage) I(FluidPDispatchArgs)))
PASS(fluidAlloc, "fluid(substep)", PT_FLUIDMAP, PIPE_FLUID_ALLOC, K_COMPUTE, 1,1,1, GRP_SLIM_FLUID, DYN_NONE, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(RW(FluidBlockMap) W(FluidBlockList) W(FluidArgsStage)))
// Same staging-copy shape as argsStage -> dispatchArgs: the indirect-only
// buffer is never bound in any bind group.
PASS(copy_fluidArgs, nullptr, PT_FLUIDMAP, PIPE_NONE, K_COPY, 0,0,12, GRP_NONE, DYN_NONE, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(TR(FluidArgsStage) TW(FluidDispatchArgs)))

// =========================================================== PT_FLUID =====
// clearGrid is no longer only a clear: it also SEEDS each node with the static
// mass of the settled liquid voxel it sits in (sim.fluidSettledMass), which is
// what makes voxel water hold particle water up. That reads the voxel field and
// the block list, exactly as gridUpdate's BC probe does, so it declares the
// same set — omitting them would leave the seeding unbarriered against the
// seam's voxel writes earlier in the tick.
// read-only in practice: `fluidBlockList` is written by alloc; the seeding only
// reads it, to turn a block index back into the chunk slot it was assigned.
PASS(fluidClear, "fluid(substep)", PT_FLUID, PIPE_FLUID_CLEAR, K_INDIRECT, IND_FLUIDARGS,0,0,
     GRP_SLIM_FLUID, DYN_NONE, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(R(Voxels) R(Materials) U(TickUBO) R(PageTable) R(FluidBlockList)
          W(FluidGrid) I(FluidDispatchArgs)))
// P2G's scatter is the plan's §5.1 bet made real: i32 atomicAdd accumulation,
// associative, so the sum cannot depend on scheduling. The fill->mark->alloc
// chain above already ordered the map; the barrier p2g1 needs is the
// clear->scatter WAW on fluidGrid, generated from the two rows' uses.
// read-only in practice: `fluidParticles` is read_write for the module (spawn
// and g2p write it); p2g1 only reads particle state to scatter it. Likewise
// `fluidBlockMap` is written by mark/alloc; p2g only atomicLoads the mapping.
PASS(fluidP2g1, "fluid(substep)", PT_FLUID, PIPE_FLUID_P2G, K_INDIRECT, IND_FLUIDPARGS,0,0, GRP_SLIM_FLUID, DYN_NONE, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(U(TickUBO) R(FluidParticlesWrite) R(FluidBlockMap) A(FluidGrid)
          R(FluidArgsStage) I(FluidPDispatchArgs)))
// p2g2 is the density-EOS half of the grantkot two-pass P2G: it needs p2g1's
// COMPLETE mass field (the A->A WAW barrier between these rows orders it),
// gathers density per particle, and scatters stress as momentum. Within the
// pass it reads only mass words and adds only to momentum words — disjoint,
// and both order-independent. RW on fluidParticles is real: p2g2 writes
// p.density (render shading reads it).
// read-only in practice: `fluidBlockMap` is written by mark/alloc; p2g2 only
// atomicLoads the mapping, exactly like p2g1 above.
PASS(fluidP2g2, "fluid(substep)", PT_FLUID, PIPE_FLUID_P2G2, K_INDIRECT, IND_FLUIDPARGS,0,0, GRP_SLIM_FLUID, DYN_NONE, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(U(TickUBO) RW(FluidParticlesWrite) R(FluidBlockMap) A(FluidGrid)
          R(FluidArgsStage) I(FluidPDispatchArgs)))
// Terrain boundary conditions read the LIVE voxel buffer through voxWordAt —
// carve under a pool and the wall simply stops being there next substep
// (plan §6.1). Reads only; the fluid never writes a voxel, which is what keeps
// the world hash out of this system's blast radius.
// read-only in practice: `fluidBlockList` is read_write for the module because
// alloc writes it; the grid update only reads its block's chunk slot.
// A(FluidBlockMap) is the Y-OCCUPANCY half (common.wgsl): a node that carries
// mass lights its y level so the RENDERER can skip empty y slabs. Render-only
// derived data, but the hazard is real — the next substep's p2g reads the index
// half of the same buffer, and the renderer reads the mask half.
// A(FluidArgsStage) is the FA_CLAMPED counter (the CFL-honesty probe): the
// only fluid substep pass that WRITES the args buffer mid-substep. The next
// consumer is the seam front of the NEXT tick (compactScan zeroes it) and the
// bench readback; the hazard edge is real either way.
PASS(fluidGridUp, "fluid(substep)", PT_FLUID, PIPE_FLUID_GRIDUP, K_INDIRECT, IND_FLUIDARGS,0,0,
     GRP_SLIM_FLUID, DYN_NONE, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(R(Voxels) R(Materials) U(TickUBO) R(PageTable) R(FluidBlockList)
          RW(FluidGrid) A(FluidBlockMap) A(FluidArgsStage)
          I(FluidDispatchArgs)))
// Pure gather for the fluid itself: each thread writes only its own particle,
// so g2p cannot race itself (the mark/apply split rule does not bite here).
// The splash coupling adds two writes: fast free-surface particles append
// PFLAG_MICRO droplets to the ballistic particle system's WRITE page (counts
// atomicAdd + slot store — the same order-independent append integrate uses;
// nothing keys on the slot). This runs AFTER particleResolve in the tick, so
// the appends land beyond the resolved count and fly next tick.
// read-only in practice: `fluidBlockMap` is written by mark/alloc and g2p
// only atomicLoads it.
// `fluidGrid` is READ-WRITE here: g2p atomicLoads the mass/velocity/species
// words, but it also atomicAdds the DIFFUSE-MATERIAL potential into word 7
// (the foam field — see the Ihmsen block in sim_fluid.wgsl). That word is the
// one grid accumulator that survives the per-substep clear, so the next
// substep's clear/p2g and the RENDERER both read what this pass wrote and the
// hazard is real, not notional.
PASS(fluidG2p, "fluid(substep)", PT_FLUID, PIPE_FLUID_G2P, K_INDIRECT, IND_FLUIDPARGS,0,0, GRP_SLIM_FLUID, DYN_NONE, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(U(TickUBO) RW(FluidParticlesWrite) R(FluidBlockMap) RW(FluidGrid)
          W(ParticlesWrite) A(ParticleCounts) R(FluidArgsStage)
          I(FluidPDispatchArgs)))

// ====================================================== PT_FLUIDSEAM =======
// The excite/settle seam, front half (sim_fluid_seam.wgsl) — recorded once
// per tick from EncodeTick, after PT_TICK and before the PT_FLUID substeps,
// only while the seam is live (particles exist, spawns arrive, or the
// disturbance-excite mode is on with an active CA). Order is load-bearing:
// compaction rebuilds the live count that spawn appends against, excite
// appends after both, and the final copies stage the per-particle dispatch
// args the substeps run on.
//
// This table WRITES VOXELS (detect's scratch bits, emit's clears) — the
// first fluid code inside the hashed world domain. Every voxel it can touch
// is in this tick's dirty list (I(DispatchArgs) domain), which §3 of the
// page-table plan guarantees is materialized.
PASS(seam_fill_excite, nullptr, PT_FLUIDSEAM, PIPE_NONE, K_FILL, 0,0,0, GRP_NONE, DYN_NONE, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(TW(FluidExciteScratch)))
PASS(seam_fill_settle, nullptr, PT_FLUIDSEAM, PIPE_NONE, K_FILL, 0,0,0, GRP_NONE, DYN_NONE, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(TW(FluidSettleScratch)))
// COMPACTION SIZING (plan §7 item 1). These two rows dispatched 1,024 fixed
// workgroups — one thread per pool SLOT, all 262,144 of them — whether 100
// particles existed or 40,000, which measured as a count-INDEPENDENT ~0.35 ms
// of seam+settle at both 6k and 40k live. They now run off
// fluidArgs[FA_ARGS_COMPACT] = ((live+255)/256, 1, 1), written by LAST tick's
// excite scan next to the live count it is derived from, and staged here.
// compactScan reads the same bound (liveSpans()) so it cannot consume a span
// this dispatch never wrote.
PASS(copy_fluidCompactArgs, nullptr, PT_FLUIDSEAM, PIPE_NONE, K_COPY, 76,0,12, GRP_NONE, DYN_NONE, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(TR(FluidArgsStage) TW(FluidPDispatchArgs)))
// read-only in practice on FluidArgsStage: the count kernels atomicLoad the
// live count; compactScan is the one that stores (and clears the per-tick
// event counters).
PASS(seam_compact_count, "fluidSeam", PT_FLUIDSEAM, PIPE_FLUID_COMPACT_COUNT, K_INDIRECT, IND_FLUIDPARGS,0,0, GRP_SLIM_FLUIDSEAM, DYN_NONE, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(R(FluidParticlesRead) A(FluidArgsStage) W(FluidCompactScratch)
          I(FluidPDispatchArgs)))
PASS(seam_compact_scan, "fluidSeam", PT_FLUIDSEAM, PIPE_FLUID_COMPACT_SCAN, K_COMPUTE, 1,1,1, GRP_SLIM_FLUIDSEAM, DYN_NONE, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(RW(FluidCompactScratch) A(FluidExciteScratch) A(FluidArgsStage)))
// read-only in practice: `compactScratch` is read_write for the module
// (compactCount/Scan write it); the scatter only reads counts and bases.
// Same indirect args as the count above — nothing between them rewrites
// fluidPDispatchArgs, so one staging copy serves both.
PASS(seam_compact_scatter, "fluidSeam", PT_FLUIDSEAM, PIPE_FLUID_COMPACT_SCATTER, K_INDIRECT, IND_FLUIDPARGS,0,0, GRP_SLIM_FLUIDSEAM, DYN_NONE, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(R(FluidParticlesRead) W(FluidParticlesWrite) R(FluidCompactScratch)
          A(FluidArgsStage) I(FluidPDispatchArgs)))
// A(FluidArgsStage): M3's FA_SPAWNDEAD counter. The water-body discharge fills a
// CPU-RESERVED op block whose tail is dead this tick, and a dead op still holds a
// pool slot FA_LIVE counts — so the count is what lets a conservation gate read
// the real in-flight mass without scanning the pool. A diagnostic counter, but it
// is still an atomic into a buffer half the fluid pipeline reads, so the row
// declares it like any other access.
PASS(seam_spawn, "fluidSeam", PT_FLUIDSEAM, PIPE_FLUID_SPAWN, K_COMPUTE, D_FLUIDSPAWN,1,1, GRP_SLIM_FLUIDSEAM, DYN_NONE, C_FLUIDSPAWN, 1,
     USES(U(TickUBO) R(FluidSpawnOps) W(FluidParticlesWrite)
          A(FluidExciteScratch) A(FluidArgsStage)))
// consumeApply's bound is the SURVIVOR count compactScan just published, so
// its args are staged here rather than with the compaction pair above.
PASS(copy_fluidConsumeArgs, nullptr, PT_FLUIDSEAM, PIPE_NONE, K_COPY, 88,0,12, GRP_NONE, DYN_NONE, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(TR(FluidArgsStage) TW(FluidPDispatchArgs)))
// read-only in practice on FluidBlockMap: the module declares it read_write
// because stainApply lights the Y-occupancy mask; consumeApply only reads the
// INDEX half to address the cell the CA flagged.
// Reactions that took excited fluid this tick (the CA's consume flags): the
// whole cell bin dies before the substeps run. Addressed via LAST substep's
// block map, exactly the addressing the CA used to set the flags.
PASS(seam_consume_apply, "fluidSeam", PT_FLUIDSEAM, PIPE_FLUID_CONSUME_APPLY, K_INDIRECT, IND_FLUIDPARGS,0,0, GRP_SLIM_FLUIDSEAM, DYN_NONE, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(U(TickUBO) RW(FluidParticlesWrite) R(FluidBlockMap)
          A(FluidCellScratch) A(FluidArgsStage) A(FluidExciteScratch)
          I(FluidPDispatchArgs)))
// read-only in practice on FluidBlockMap: read_write for the module (stainApply
// writes the Y-occupancy mask); the wake probe only reads the index half.
// C_CAACTIVE: detect runs over THIS tick's compacted dirty list; when the CA
// proved the world settled the list was not rebuilt and there is nothing
// disturbed to excite.
// A(FluidArgsStage): the WP5 excite-REACH probe (FA_EXSEEN / FA_EXCANDID).
// Diagnostic counters, but they are still atomics into a buffer half the fluid
// pipeline reads, so the row declares them like any other access — an omitted
// read here is an invisible cross-vendor hazard, not a missing statistic.
// A(WaterBodyState): COMPONENT 7's drain shell (PLAN_water_master.md M3). The
// trigger reads a draining hole's position, level and granted discharge out of
// the ledger the water-body rows wrote earlier in the same tick, and adds the
// cells it converts into WBS_EXSHELL — the measurement plan §9 item 2 calls
// for. It is a cross-TABLE hazard (PT_TICK writes, PT_FLUIDSEAM reads), which
// is exactly the kind an undeclared access makes invisible.
PASS(seam_excite_detect, "fluidSeam", PT_FLUIDSEAM, PIPE_FLUID_EXCITE_DETECT, K_INDIRECT, IND_DISPATCHARGS,0,0, GRP_SLIM_FLUIDSEAM, DYN_NONE, C_CAACTIVE, 1,
     USES(RW(Voxels) R(Materials) U(TickUBO) R(PageTable) R(DirtyList)
          R(FluidBlockMap) R(FluidGrid) A(FluidExciteScratch)
          A(FluidArgsStage) A(WaterBodyState) I(DispatchArgs)))
PASS(seam_excite_scan, "fluidSeam", PT_FLUIDSEAM, PIPE_FLUID_EXCITE_SCAN, K_COMPUTE, 1,1,1, GRP_SLIM_FLUIDSEAM, DYN_NONE, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(U(TickUBO) A(FluidExciteScratch) A(FluidArgsStage)))
// The emit dispatch is list-shaped (one workgroup per candidate slot):
// stage its args from the excite scratch header.
PASS(copy_exciteArgs, nullptr, PT_FLUIDSEAM, PIPE_NONE, K_COPY, 16,0,12, GRP_NONE, DYN_NONE, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(TR(FluidExciteScratch) TW(FluidPDispatchArgs)))
PASS(seam_excite_emit, "fluidSeam", PT_FLUIDSEAM, PIPE_FLUID_EXCITE_EMIT, K_INDIRECT, IND_FLUIDPARGS,0,0, GRP_SLIM_FLUIDSEAM, DYN_NONE, C_CAACTIVE, 1,
     USES(RW(Voxels) U(TickUBO) R(PageTable) A(DirtyOut)
          A(FluidExciteScratch) A(FluidArgsStage)
          W(FluidParticlesWrite) I(FluidPDispatchArgs)))
// Per-particle dispatch args for the substeps and the settle half —
// (live + 63) / 64, written by the excite scan into fluidArgs[4..6].
PASS(copy_fluidPArgs, nullptr, PT_FLUIDSEAM, PIPE_NONE, K_COPY, 16,0,12, GRP_NONE, DYN_NONE, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(TR(FluidArgsStage) TW(FluidPDispatchArgs)))

// ==================================================== PT_FLUIDSETTLE =======
// The seam's back half, recorded once per tick after the PT_FLUID substeps:
// judge calm, pick blocks, bin, feasibility-check, commit voxels, kill
// particles. All voxel writes land in >= 8-tick-calm fluid-block chunks,
// which the block-list readback has fed to PageTable::UpdateFluidChunks for
// at least that long.
// The intent/flags scratch clears HERE, at the head of the settle phase:
// the CA has already read last tick's intents and consumeApply has already
// read this tick's flags, and particleTick refills the intents next.
// Was an 8 MiB whole-buffer FILL, every tick, forever — the buffer is sized by
// the kFluidBlocks CEILING (256 blocks x 4096 cells x 2 words) while the lab
// scenes measure 8-22 active blocks. Now a compute clear over the LAST
// substep's active blocks (plan §7 item 1), which is also zero work when the
// solver is asleep.
PASS(seam_cell_clear, "fluidSettle", PT_FLUIDSETTLE, PIPE_FLUID_CELL_CLEAR, K_INDIRECT, IND_FLUIDARGS,0,0,
     GRP_SLIM_FLUIDSEAM, DYN_NONE, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(A(FluidCellScratch) I(FluidDispatchArgs)))
// read-only in practice: `fluidParticles` is read_write for the module
// (compaction/spawn/emit/kill write it); particleTick only reads state to
// fold each particle's speed into its slot's maximum and scatter its
// occupancy + contact-stain intents.
PASS(seam_particle_tick, "fluidSettle", PT_FLUIDSETTLE, PIPE_FLUID_PTICK, K_INDIRECT, IND_FLUIDPARGS,0,0, GRP_SLIM_FLUIDSEAM, DYN_NONE, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(U(TickUBO) R(FluidParticlesWrite) A(FluidSettleScratch)
          A(FluidArgsStage) R(Voxels) R(Materials) R(PageTable)
          R(FluidBlockMap) A(FluidCellScratch) I(FluidPDispatchArgs)))
// Contact staining: one thread per active-block cell (the node-pass args of
// the LAST substep), applying the intents particleTick just scattered —
// solid surfaces touched by excited fluid take its stain, the CA's
// doStaining semantics on the particle side.
// A(FluidBlockMap): the settled-liquid half of the Y-occupancy mask. This is
// the one pass that already walks every active-block cell with the voxel word
// in hand, and settled liquid contributes virtual mass to the isosurface, so
// without it the march would skip the y levels a half-converted pool holds as
// voxels.
PASS(seam_stain_apply, "fluidSettle", PT_FLUIDSETTLE, PIPE_FLUID_STAIN_APPLY, K_INDIRECT, IND_FLUIDARGS,0,0, GRP_SLIM_FLUIDSEAM, DYN_NONE, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(RW(Voxels) R(Materials) U(TickUBO) R(PageTable) A(DirtyOut)
          R(FluidBlockList) A(FluidCellScratch) A(FluidArgsStage)
          A(FluidBlockMap) I(FluidDispatchArgs)))
PASS(seam_settle_judge, "fluidSettle", PT_FLUIDSETTLE, PIPE_FLUID_SETTLE_JUDGE, K_COMPUTE, 128,1,1, GRP_SLIM_FLUIDSEAM, DYN_NONE, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(A(FluidSettleScratch) A(FluidCalm)))
PASS(seam_settle_scan, "fluidSettle", PT_FLUIDSETTLE, PIPE_FLUID_SETTLE_SCAN, K_COMPUTE, 1,1,1, GRP_SLIM_FLUIDSEAM, DYN_NONE, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(A(FluidCalm) A(FluidSettleScratch) A(FluidArgsStage)))
// read-only in practice: `fluidParticles` is read_write for the module;
// settleBin only reads position + attr to bin eighths and stains.
PASS(seam_settle_bin, "fluidSettle", PT_FLUIDSETTLE, PIPE_FLUID_SETTLE_BIN, K_INDIRECT, IND_FLUIDPARGS,0,0, GRP_SLIM_FLUIDSEAM, DYN_NONE, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(U(TickUBO) R(FluidParticlesWrite) A(FluidSettleScratch)
          A(FluidArgsStage) I(FluidPDispatchArgs)))
// check runs the identical column walk as commit with write=false — it reads
// voxels and never takes the write branches, but the branches are statically
// reachable through the shared settleColumn, so the row declares the same
// write set (a too-wide barrier is pure cost; a too-narrow one is a race).
// Any infeasible column refuses its whole block, which is what makes the
// commit's writes all-or-nothing per block.
// A(FluidBlockMap) R(FluidGrid): the second test — excite stability, WP3's
// hysteresis-by-construction — asks what a lateral neighbour cell HOLDS, and
// for the water next door that is still particles the answer is node mass, not
// the (empty) voxel. Same block-map + Q10-mass read exciteDetect's wake probe
// and mirrorFold already make. Read-only here; A() because the module binds
// the map read_write for stainApply's Y-mask.
PASS(seam_settle_check, "fluidSettle", PT_FLUIDSETTLE, PIPE_FLUID_SETTLE_CHECK, K_COMPUTE, 16,1,1, GRP_SLIM_FLUIDSEAM, DYN_NONE, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(RW(Voxels) R(Materials) U(TickUBO) R(PageTable) A(DirtyOut)
          A(FluidSettleScratch) A(FluidCalm) A(FluidArgsStage)
          A(FluidBlockMap) R(FluidGrid)))
PASS(seam_settle_commit, "fluidSettle", PT_FLUIDSETTLE, PIPE_FLUID_SETTLE_COMMIT, K_COMPUTE, 16,1,1, GRP_SLIM_FLUIDSEAM, DYN_NONE, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(RW(Voxels) R(Materials) U(TickUBO) R(PageTable) A(DirtyOut)
          A(FluidSettleScratch) A(FluidCalm) A(FluidArgsStage)))
PASS(seam_settle_kill, "fluidSettle", PT_FLUIDSETTLE, PIPE_FLUID_SETTLE_KILL, K_INDIRECT, IND_FLUIDPARGS,0,0, GRP_SLIM_FLUIDSEAM, DYN_NONE, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(U(TickUBO) RW(FluidParticlesWrite) A(FluidSettleScratch)
          A(FluidArgsStage) I(FluidPDispatchArgs)))
// read-only in practice on FluidBlockMap: read_write for the module (stainApply
// writes the Y-occupancy mask); the fold only reads the index half.
// The swimming fold: excited-fluid eighths for the 27 CPU-mirror chunks,
// packed for the snapshot readback (plan §6.5). Last so it reads the tick's
// final node mass.
PASS(seam_mirror_fold, "fluidSettle", PT_FLUIDSETTLE, PIPE_FLUID_MIRROR_FOLD, K_COMPUTE, 27,1,1, GRP_SLIM_FLUIDSEAM, DYN_NONE, C_ALWAYS, 1,
     USES(U(TickUBO) R(FluidBlockMap) R(FluidGrid) W(FluidMirror)))

